  "step_3": "The decision reason requires assessing the skewness of the lottery distributions, particularly favoring the one more heavily weighted toward unfavorable outcomes (more right-skewed). This involves recognizing that a lottery with a low chance of a high payoff and high chance of nothing (like A) is right-skewed. However, the individual instead focuses on the higher probability of receiving any money, which corresponds to preferring left-skewed lotteries (like B). The information considered by the individual (probability of non-zero outcome) is partially related to skewness but interpreted in the opposite direction—favoring higher chance of gain, not higher chance of loss. The justification contradicts the rule of the decision reason, which would favor A due to its greater right-skewness. There is no evidence the individual evaluated distribution asymmetry or consciously preferred right-skewness. Therefore, it is certain that the individual did not use this decision reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
