  "step_3": "The decision reason relies on assessing skewness by comparing the probability mass on unfavorable outcomes. It prefers the lottery that is more right-skewed, defined as having more probability on unfavorable outcomes. In this case, that would be Lottery A (0.2% vs 0.1%), so the reason would favor A. The individual, however, cites a higher chance of getting 0 Euros, which would actually favor Lottery B, not A. This suggests either a factual error or a different focus. The individual is focused on the probability of the neutral (0 Euro) outcome, not on the skewness or the balance between favorable and unfavorable probabilities per se. The reasoning does not mention the size of the loss or the distributional shape (skewness), nor does it compare the probabilities of losses directly. Instead, it emphasizes the chance of no loss, which is conceptually related but distinct from skewness as defined. There is no indication that the individual assessed which lottery has more probability on unfavorable outcomes (which would be A) or used that to justify the choice. The justification is factually inaccurate if used to compare 0 Euro outcomes, and does not align with the skewness-based rule. Therefore, while both consider probabilities of outcomes, the specific logic and comparison do not match the decision reason.",
