  "step_3": "The decision reason prefers the more right-skewed lottery, which is Lottery A (due to its concentration of probability on an unfavorable outcome—0 Euros—with only a tiny chance of a large gain). However, the individual chooses Lottery B, which is less right-skewed (in fact, not skewed at all). More importantly, while the individual does consider the high probability of an unfavorable outcome in Lottery A (99.9% of 0 Euros), their reasoning leads them to reject, not prefer, the more right-skewed option. The decision reason would favor Lottery A for being more right-skewed, but the individual avoids it for exactly that reason. Thus, although the individual considers the distribution of probabilities (relevant to skewness), they use this information in the opposite way to the decision reason: they avoid high skewness rather than prefer it. Therefore, the individual is not applying the decision reason; they are in fact reasoning against it.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

